  "worked_solution": "Let's introduce the new variable $u={e}^{x}+4.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{x}\\sqrt{{e}^{x}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\sqrt{{e}^{x}+4}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\int {u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\sqrt{({e}^{x}+4)^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
